# The Vercel project's Root Directory is "web", so a deploy is made from HERE -- the repo
# root -- and Vercel then builds web/. But this root is a pokeemerald fork: two 16 MB
# ROMs, a build tree, the whole of Hoenn's graphics and audio. None of that is the site,
# and one of those ROMs is somebody else's copyright.
#
# EVERY PATH HERE IS ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, and that is load-bearing. A bare
# `src` is a gitignore pattern that matches at any depth, so it takes web/src with it and
# the build dies on "Failed to resolve /src/app.ts". Same for data, tools, docs, build,
# include and constants -- web/ has its own of several of those.
#
# The earlier `/*` + `!/web/` form does not work either: it left web/ out of the upload
# altogether and Vercel answered "the specified Root Directory web does not exist", which
# is a confusing way to be told the site was never sent.
/.git
/.github
/.claude
/asm
/build
/constants
/data
/docs
/graphics
/include
/libagbsyscall
/sound
/src
/tools
/relay
/*.elf
/*.map
/*.mk
/*.ld
/*.md
/Makefile
/charmap.txt
/rom.sha1
/sym_*.txt
/asmdiff.*
/build_tools.sh

# ...and the parts of web/ that are not the site. NOT spike/, which is a build input
# (vite.config.ts's rollupOptions), and NOT public/patch, which is the whole point:
# web/public/patch/* is gitignored because the patch is a build artifact, and Vercel
# falls back to .gitignore when there is no .vercelignore. That is why the first deploy
# went up with no BPS at all and the shell told everybody "no patch published yet".
/web/node_modules
/web/dist
/web/.vercel
/web/test-results
/web/playwright-report
/web/e2e
/web/.env*

# The dev ROM, and anything ROM-shaped anywhere -- these stay unanchored on purpose.
# tools/br/dev-patch.sh leaves a full, patched Emerald in web/public/patch/ so the dev
# shell can skip the file picker; app.ts fetches it only under import.meta.env.DEV and
# its own comment says "nothing ships this file". The build drops it too
# (vite.config.ts's br-drop-roms) and web/scripts/no-rom.mjs asserts the result. Three
# locks on one door: the ROM belongs on the player's device and nowhere else.
*.gba
*.sav
*.ss?
